sections.body:
Building a house takes a few months. Living in it takes decades, and over those years the heating, the repairs and the new wiring cost far more than the building did. Software is the same. The payments company's nightly payout job, which adds up what each shop took that day, might be written in three months and then run every night for ten years. In those years, people fix its bugs, keep it running, find out why it was slow last Tuesday, move it to new machines and add new ways to pay. Most of the cost of software is in that work. And every system useful enough to survive becomes somebody's legacy system: old code that people must keep running long after its authors have moved on.

Three properties keep that cost down: operability, simplicity and evolvability.

**Operability** means the people who look after a system can keep it running. In most teams, one person at a time is on call: it's their turn to be woken if the system breaks. A data system helps them when it:

- reports metrics (numbers about itself, such as requests per second), logs (lines saying what it did) and traces (the path of one request through every program it touched);
- doesn't depend on any single machine, so they can take one down to repair it;
- works in a way you can say in one breath: "if I do X, Y happens";
- comes with sensible default settings that can be changed;
- fixes common problems by itself, but lets a person take control;
- behaves predictably, so nobody is surprised at 3 a.m.

The operators' tools count too. Faults, failures and the people who run systems told the story of S3 in February 2017. S3 is the file-storage service of AWS, Amazon's business that rents out computers over the internet. One mistyped input to a command that removes servers took S3 down in Amazon's northern Virginia region for about four hours. The engineer had followed the written procedure, and the tool let one typo remove far more servers than anyone meant to. So AWS put the fix into the tool: it now removes servers slowly, and refuses to take any part of S3 below the minimum it needs.

**A warning in a document doesn't stop a typo. Put the guardrail in the tool.**

Automation, letting a program do the routine steps, cuts both ways. It leaves people only the rare, hard cases, which they now get less practice at, and when it goes wrong it's harder to follow than a person running each step. So automate the routine, keep a manual override for every automatic action, and make the automation say what it's doing and why.

Defaults matter as well. Most teams never change most settings, so the default is the setting. A program that waits forever for a free connection to its database (the program that stores its data), or retries a failed request without limit, becomes a problem on somebody else's night.

The written procedure for one alarm has a name: a **runbook**, like the card by the boiler that says which valve to close. A good one says what the alarm means, how to confirm it, the safe first action, how to undo that action, and when to call for help. A runbook is only as good as the alarm that sends you to it, though, so next we'll build alarms worth trusting.

